Calculating the LSB of bit runs
This function can be achieved with x & ~(x &lt;&lt; 1). It is based on the fact that the LSB of every bit run is preceded by an unset bit (or will be after a left shift). Zeros become ones when inverted, so after shifting and inverting, the only set bits in both the original and manipulated bitset are the LSB of each bit run. Calling this function on a flat bitmask (that we’ve already filtered to only contain real plateaus) gives us the plateau locations.

